<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yi Hwang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1501">1501</a>–<a href="/years/1570">1570</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Toegye</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yi Hwang, known as Toegye, was one of the two greatest Neo-Confucian philosophers of Joseon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. After a career as an official he retired to teach at his academy at Dosan, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andong">Andong</a>. His interpretation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a>, set out in works such as the Ten Diagrams on Sage Learning and in a famous debate on the Four-Seven question, influenced later Korean thought and Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a>, and he appears on the South Korean 1,000-won note.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yi-hwang.jpg" alt="Yi Hwang" width="320" height="566" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Integral · CC BY-SA 2.0 kr · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Yi_Hwang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
